A||N||3||KRMFiero||Sanding Fiero/Body Prep?||||1||
Z||000000||KRMFiero||07-30-2001||11:55 PM||||Ahh This will be hard to do for me, scrach my baby...<P>I have to atleast get my Car in primer the paint is starting to chip and stuff.. First What do I use to sand it with? Block? Circular air sander thing? Does it always have to be wet sanded or what? <BR>What Grades of sand paper do I use?<BR>Do I sand the same way on spolers, decklids,ground effects?<BR>What is the best Primer to use? how much will i need?<P>Any tips? words of advise?<BR>Kyle<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||DecadenceR||07-31-2001||01:02 AM||||I personally only know the answer to part of that...<BR>YOu do want to wet-sand it. it helps you get a clean, nice sand plus it keeps the sandpaper clean so you dont have to use a whole case for one fender. At least that's how my friend's cousin, a class-A body man that used to do only vettes explained it to me when I was 16.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Devotshka||07-31-2001||01:10 AM||||Wetsanding is my personal fav, just be sure to go in straight motions and in the same direction.... doesn't really matter much if you are respraying, but its a good habit to get into when you start polishing.  <BR>240wet will cut it pretty fast, i prefer 400 myself.  You should use a block, prefeably stiff foam, as your fingers will leave dips in the finish that you'll see in dark paintjobs.<P>If you use an air/dual action sander, it will take a whole lot of sandpaper, but will get the job done much faster (IMO)... Since your paint is peeling, you are going to need to get all the way through the peeling layer, optimally anyway.<P>From most primers i've used, you should not spray them until you are ready to lay your basecoat.  Lots of them are meant to be still a little soft when the base coat goes on.  Not sure if that holds true for all primers though.<BR>Of course if you're using spraybombs to prime it, never mind that last tidbit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-31-2001||02:32 PM||||400 is plenty fine enough for a repaint. I prefer dry sanding because I usually paint a car the next day or so and water has a nasty habit of dripping out on the last finish coat. Wet sandings the best to use the least paper. Contrary to what you may have heard, wet sanding wont make it any smoother though. And yes, if its peeling or clouded up anywhere you have to get it off down to the base paint or plastic. If you dont ,when new paint dries it will leave an outline of it in the finish, or even wrinkle there.<P>on primer. If you can find it use laquer primer-surfacer, it dries fast and is ez to sand. ez to see defects. second choice would be an epoxy base primer.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||1
